#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Manage resume actions, completed-task history, and the session-task queue.

Three distinct layers of task coordination:

  1. session-task set/clear -- single "top-of-mind" slot for the NEXT resume
     action. There is only one at a time. Lives in resume-action.json.

  2. session-task queue ... -- a cross-session work queue with scope-based
     serialization groups. Any number of items. Items in the same scope group
     run one-at-a-time (priority + FIFO). Items in disjoint groups run in
     parallel. Lives in queue.json (with fcntl.flock on every RMW).

  3. TaskCreate / TaskList / TaskUpdate (harness primitive, not in this tool)
     -- tracks background PROCESSES (unix agents/watchers/scans).

Completed items from both layers 1 and 2 are logged to completed-tasks.jsonl.

Usage:
    session-task set "description"
    session-task get | show | clear | append "..."
    session-task complete "description"
    session-task history [-n N]

    session-task queue add "desc" --scope file:~/foo --scope repo:bar \\
        [--summary "~10 word headline"] [--priority N] [--context JSON] \\
        [--created-by WHO] [--depends-on q-AAA,q-BBB]
    session-task queue list [--ready] [--all] [--group g-X] [--json]
    session-task queue show <id>
    session-task queue scope <id>
    session-task queue groups [--json]
    session-task queue ready [--json]
    session-task queue done <id>
    session-task queue abandon <id> [--reason TEXT] [--confirmed-dead [--force]]
                                    # running/wedged -> QUARANTINED (scope
                                    # stays locked); pending/blocked ->
                                    # abandoned. --confirmed-dead skips
                                    # quarantine, for callers with an actual
                                    # exit code.
    session-task queue release <id> [--reason TEXT] [--force] [--json]
                                    # quarantined -> abandoned; frees the
                                    # scope. THE unwedge command.
    session-task queue resurrect <old-id> [--reason TEXT] [--summary TEXT]
                                          [--keep-old] [--from-transcript PATH]
                                          [--created-by WHO] [--json]
    session-task queue pop [--id ID]
    session-task queue register <id> [--pid N]   # claim item for spawn (gated)
    session-task queue heartbeat <id> [--pid N]  # refresh liveness on running item
    session-task queue wedge <id> --reason TEXT  # flag a stuck running item
    session-task queue unwedge <id>              # mark wedged item recovered
    session-task queue block <id> --reason TEXT  # park a running item on an
                                                 # external blocker (human
                                                 # decision, third-party API,
                                                 # branch-protection, etc.)
    session-task queue unblock <id>              # blocked -> running
    session-task queue spawn-check <id> [--json] # "am I clear to spawn?" check
    session-task queue force-start <id> --reason TEXT  # MANUAL OVERRIDE: promote
                                                       # blocked-pending to running
    session-task queue promote <id>
    session-task queue set-summary <id> "text"   # edit one-line summary
    session-task queue update <id> [--description TEXT | --desc-file FILE]
                                   [--summary TEXT] [--scope TOK ...] [--force]
                                   # edit a NOT-YET-SPAWNED item's task spec in
                                   # place (description/summary/scope) instead
                                   # of abandon+re-add; refuses if a LIVE agent
                                   # is bound (override with --force)
    session-task queue set-block-reason <id> --reason TEXT  # update the
                                                 # block_reason on an already-
                                                 # blocked item, in place
                                                 # (preserves blocked_at)
    session-task queue depend <id> [--add q-AAA[,q-BBB]] [--remove q-CCC]
                                   [--clear] [--json]
    session-task queue prune [--older-than DURATION] [-n/--dry-run]
    session-task queue banner
    session-task queue lock <scope> [--reason "..."]   # park items overlapping
                                                       # <scope> until unlocked
    session-task queue unlock <scope>                  # release the lock
    session-task queue locks                           # list currently locked
                                                       # scopes
    session-task queue migrate --from FILE [--yes]

Scope tokens (2026-05-08): scope is a list of `<type>:<value>` tokens that
serve as the single substrate for serialization, group-merge, locks, AND
explicit cross-queue dependencies. Token types:
    file:<path>       prefix-match (`**` suffix is readability sugar)
    repo:<name>       exact-match -- claims the WHOLE repo; overlaps any
                      `path:<name>/...` token in the same repo.
    path:<repo>/<subdir>
                      finer-grained claim WITHIN a repo (added 2026-05-11
                      to fix over-serialization when work in disjoint
                      subtrees of the same repo collides on `repo:`).
                      Prefix-match within the same repo: `path:X/a` and
                      `path:X/a/b` overlap, siblings `path:X/a` vs
                      `path:X/b` do NOT. Cross-repo `path:X/a` vs
                      `path:Y/a` never overlap. `repo:X` overlaps EVERY
                      `path:X/...` (the broad claim still wins).
    resource:<name>   exact-match
    book:<name>       exact-match
    agent-proto:<name> exact-match
    task:<queue-id>   inert dep marker -- carries the semantic "this item
                      is blocked until queue id <queue-id> is done", BUT
                      it does NOT serialize via group-merge or
                      running-conflict (so two items both depending on
                      q-A don't accidentally serialize each other). The
                      lock machinery still operates on it as a regular
                      token, so `queue lock task:<id>` parks every
                      pending item depending on <id>.
    *                 universal -- overlaps everything; use sparingly.

`--depends-on q-A` is parser sugar for appending `task:q-A` to scope --
the canonical surface is the scope token, not a separate field. Legacy
`depends_on` fields on disk are migrated to task: scope tokens on read.

Spawn gating (2026-04-17):
    Before spawning an agent, the main loop MUST go through the queue:
        1. `session-task queue add "desc" --scope <toks>` -> get id + ready_now
        2. If ready_now=false, DO NOT spawn. Wait for blockers to clear.
        3. `session-task queue spawn-check <id>` -> ok|conflict
        4. `session-task queue register <id>` -> atomically claim (running)
        5. Spawn the agent.
        6. On completion: `session-task queue done <id>` (or abandon).
    `queue register` refuses if the item isn't pending+ready, or if any
    other running item has overlapping scope (cross-group conflict).
    This is the one enforcement point. Bypassing it == operating outside
    the queue, which Andrew has flagged as a rule violation.

Abandoning a live scope (quarantine):
    `queue abandon` on a running/wedged item does NOT free its scope. It
    moves the item to `quarantined`, which still blocks a spawn on that
    scope, because "the agent looks dead" is an inference and inferences
    have been wrong (an agent presumed dead ran another ~48 minutes while
    a replacement redid its work; a duplicate reached a user). End a
    quarantine with `queue done` (the agent finished), `queue resurrect`
    (respawn a replacement -- scope carries over, so a genuinely dead
    agent costs no extra step) or `queue release` (you assert it's gone).
    See the "Quarantine" comment block below for the full rationale.
"""

